1141:
The commission shall be authorized to:
(1) Prepare and maintain an inventory of all property within the city limits having the potential for designation as historic properties;
(2) Recommend to the mayor and board of aldermen specific places, districts, sites, buildings, structures or works of art to be designated by ordinance as historic properties or historic districts;
(3) Review and grant or deny applications for certificates of appropriateness in accordance with the provisions of this article;
(4) Recommend to the mayor and board of aldermen that the designation of any place, district, site, building, structure or work of art as a historic property or historic district be revoked or removed;
(5) Restore or preserve any historic properties acquired by the city with the approval of the mayor and board of aldermen;
(6) Promote the acquisition by the city of facade easements and conservation easements in accordance with the provisions of the "Facade and Conservation Easements Act of 1976" (O.C.G.A. § 44-10-1 et seq.);
(7) Conduct educational programs on historic properties located within the city limits;
(8) Make such investigations and studies of matters relating to historic preservation as the mayor and board of aldermen or the commission itself may deem necessary or appropriate for the purpose of preserving resources, including but not limited to review and comment on actions by the city against unsafe or unfit dwellings, buildings or structures pursuant to article III, chapter 18;
(9) Seek out state and federal funds for historic preservation and outline a program for the mayor and board of aldermen concerning the most appropriate uses of any funds acquired;
(10) Submit to the historic preservation section of the state department of natural resources a list of historic landmarks or historic districts proposed to be designated and a list of historic landmarks and historic districts designated;
(11) Perform historic preservation activities as the official agency of the city historic preservation program, with consent from the mayor and board of aldermen;
(12) Receive donations, grants, funds or gifts of historic properties, and acquire and sell historic properties, with the approval of the mayor and board of aldermen; the commission shall not obligate the mayor and board of aldermen in any respect, financially or otherwise, without prior consent of the mayor and board of aldermen;
(13) Consult with historic preservation experts in the historic preservation section of the state department of natural resources or its successor and the Georgia Trust for Historic Preservation, Inc.;
(14) Review and make comments to the state historic preservation office concerning the nomination of properties within the city limits to the National Register of Historic Places;
(15) Employ persons, if necessary, to carry out the responsibilities of the commission with prior approval from the mayor and board of aldermen; and
(16) Participate in private, state and federal historic preservation programs and, with the consent of the mayor and board of aldermen, enter into agreements to do so.
(Code 1983, § 15-34(d); Ord. No. 97-010, 7-17-1997)

